"""Scene definitions for the The Principle of Least Privilege teaching video (generated from concept.toml)."""

SCENES = [
    dict(key='01-poster', kind='poster', title='The Principle of Least Privilege',
         body=None,
         narration="Hello, and welcome. [[slnc 400]] This video explains the principle of least privilege, in Java. [[slnc 300]] This video is presented by Jayasekhar Konduru. [[slnc 600]] First, a simple definition. [[slnc 300]] Every person and every program should have only the permissions its work needs, for only as long as it needs them. [[slnc 600]] Think of a hotel. [[slnc 300]] A guest's key opens one room and stops working at checkout. [[slnc 300]] A master key is kept in a safe. [[slnc 300]] If a guest's key is stolen, one room is lost, not the hotel. [[slnc 700]] In this video, the domain is an online store. [[slnc 500]] By the end, you will hear how to measure the damage a stolen credential can do. [[slnc 300]] Why two people should be needed for a refund. [[slnc 300]] And why access should expire by itself."),
    dict(key='02-scenario', kind='bullets', title='The Scenario',
         body=['An online store has staff and unattended jobs.', '', 'It is quickest to make everything an admin.', '', 'Then every account is worth stealing.'],
         narration='Here is the scenario. [[slnc 400]] An online store has staff, and unattended jobs, and they all need some access. [[slnc 500]] It is quickest to make everything an admin, so that nothing ever fails. [[slnc 600]] And then every account is worth stealing.'),
    dict(key='03-words', kind='bullets', title='The Words',
         body=['Privilege: a permission to do something', 'Role: a bundle of permissions', 'Deny by default: nothing allowed unless stated', 'Blast radius: what one stolen credential reaches', 'Separation of duties: two people for risky acts'],
         narration='Before the demos, the words. [[slnc 500]] A privilege is a permission to do something. [[slnc 500]] A role is a bundle of permissions. [[slnc 500]] Deny by default means nothing is allowed unless a statement allows it. [[slnc 500]] The blast radius is what one stolen credential can reach. [[slnc 500]] And separation of duties means two people are needed for a risky act.'),
    dict(key='04-letters-1', kind='bullets', title='What the Letters Stand For',
         body=['JDK: Java Development Kit: Java and its tools', 'IAM: Identity and Access Management: cloud permissions', 'R-1, R-2: refund 1, refund 2: the refunds in act three', 'rwx: read, write, execute: file permission letters', 'POSIX: Portable Operating System Interface: the Unix standard', '*: star, a wildcard: matches anything'],
         narration='A few letters come up again and again, so here is what they stand for. [[slnc 400]] J D K is the Java Development Kit: Java, and the tools to build and run it. [[slnc 400]] I A M is identity and access management: how a cloud decides who may do what. [[slnc 400]] R one and R two are the numbers of two refunds in the store. [[slnc 400]] R, W and X are read, write, and execute. On a folder, x means you may enter it. A dash means not allowed. [[slnc 400]] POSIX is the Portable Operating System Interface: the Unix standard that macOS and Linux follow, including file permissions. [[slnc 400]] And a star is a wildcard. Allow star allows everything. Deny secrets star denies every action on the secrets.'),
    dict(key='05-who-can-do-what', kind='figure', title='Who can do what in the store',
         body='role_matrix',
         narration="Let's see the whole idea on one picture. [[slnc 400]] Down the left are the fifteen things that can be done in the store. [[slnc 300]] Read the orders, approve a refund, read the secrets, and so on. [[slnc 500]] Across the top are four roles. [[slnc 300]] A filled box means the role allows it. [[slnc 600]] The admin column, in red, is filled from top to bottom. [[slnc 300]] Fifteen of fifteen. [[slnc 500]] Support, warehouse and finance, in green, each allow three. [[slnc 300]] Just what that job needs. [[slnc 600]] Here is why it matters. [[slnc 300]] A stolen key opens exactly what its role allows. [[slnc 300]] A stolen support password reaches three things. [[slnc 300]] A stolen admin password reaches everything. [[slnc 500]] And any box that is empty is denied, by default. [[slnc 300]] Next, the five demos."),
    dict(key='06-act-1-figure', kind='figure', title='How a request is decided, statement by statement',
         body='decisions',
         narration='First demo: how a request is decided. [[slnc 400]] There are two requests, side by side. [[slnc 600]] On the left, in blue, the request. [[slnc 300]] Support asks to approve a refund. [[slnc 400]] Below it, the check. [[slnc 300]] The support role has three statements. [[slnc 300]] Allow orders read. [[slnc 200]] Allow customers read. [[slnc 200]] Allow refunds create. [[slnc 400]] None of them matches refunds approve. [[slnc 400]] So the decision, in red, is deny by default. [[slnc 300]] Nothing allows it, so it is denied. [[slnc 700]] On the right, an admin, with one extra statement: deny secrets star. [[slnc 300]] The request is to read the secrets. [[slnc 400]] The first statement, allow star, matches everything. [[slnc 300]] So far, it would be allowed. [[slnc 400]] But the second statement, deny secrets star, matches as well. [[slnc 300]] And a deny always wins. [[slnc 400]] The decision is deny explicit. [[slnc 600]] Two rules, then. [[slnc 300]] What nothing allows is denied. [[slnc 300]] And a deny beats any allow.'),
    dict(key='07-act-1-1', kind='console', title='Act One — Roles and permissions, and deny by default',
         body='ONE. Roles and permissions, and deny by default.\n  THE CATALOGUE (15 things that can be done in the store):\n    orders     read  write  delete\n    customers  read  write  delete\n    payments   read  write\n    refunds    create  approve\n    inventory  read  write\n    reports    read\n    secrets    read  write\n  THE ROLES (what each one allows):\n    admin      15 of 15  *\n    support    3 of 15   orders:read customers:read refunds:create\n    warehouse  3 of 15   inventory:read inventory:write orders:read\n    finance    3 of 15   payments:read refunds:approve reports:read',
         narration='Here is the real output of the program. [[slnc 400]] First, the catalogue: fifteen things that can be done in the store, grouped by what they act on. [[slnc 500]] Then the roles. [[slnc 300]] Admin allows all fifteen, with one star. [[slnc 300]] Support, warehouse and finance allow three each.'),
    dict(key='08-act-1-2', kind='console', title='Act One — Roles and permissions, and deny by default',
         body='ONE. (continued)\n  THE REQUEST: support asks for refunds:approve\n  THE CHECK, STATEMENT BY STATEMENT:\n    allow orders:read     does not match\n    allow customers:read  does not match\n    allow refunds:create  does not match\n  THE DECISION: DENY_DEFAULT  <- NOTHING ALLOWS IT\n  THE REQUEST: an admin with deny secrets:* asks for secrets:read\n  THE CHECK, STATEMENT BY STATEMENT:\n    allow *               matches: allowed, unless a deny matches\n    deny secrets:*        matches: a deny, which wins\n  THE DECISION: DENY_EXPLICIT  <- A DENY BEATS ANY ALLOW',
         narration="Now the two requests. [[slnc 300]] For each one, the request, then every statement checked against it, then the decision. [[slnc 500]] Support's three statements do not match, so deny by default. [[slnc 500]] For the admin, allow star matches, deny secrets star matches too, and the deny wins."),
    dict(key='09-act-2-figure', kind='figure', title='The blast radius of one stolen token',
         body='blast',
         narration="Second demo: the blast radius. [[slnc 400]] The blast radius is everything one stolen credential can reach. [[slnc 600]] Each box is one of the store's fifteen permissions. [[slnc 300]] A filled box is one the token reaches. [[slnc 600]] On the left, in red, before the fix. [[slnc 300]] The nightly order export runs as an admin, because that was quick. [[slnc 400]] Every box is filled. [[slnc 300]] A thief with that token can delete customers, write payments, and read the secrets. [[slnc 600]] The two green boxes are all the job really needs: read the orders, and read the reports. [[slnc 600]] On the right, in green, after the fix. [[slnc 300]] The same job, with only the exporter role. [[slnc 300]] Only those two boxes are filled. [[slnc 400]] Two of fifteen. [[slnc 600]] The job works exactly as before. [[slnc 300]] The other thirteen were risk, without any benefit."),
    dict(key='10-act-2', kind='console', title='Act Two — Blast radius: what a stolen credential can reach',
         body='TWO. Blast radius: what a stolen credential can reach.\n  WHAT THE NIGHTLY ORDER EXPORT NEEDS:\n    orders:read  reports:read\n  BEFORE THE FIX (the job runs as admin, because it was quick):\n    a stolen token reaches 15 of 15  <- EVERYTHING\n    13 of them the job never needs\n  AFTER THE FIX (the same job with only the exporter role):\n    a stolen token reaches 2 of 15\n    0 of them the job never needs\n  ANOTHER ADMIN, WITH AN EXPLICIT deny secrets:*:\n    a stolen token reaches 13 of 15\n  whatever the job does not need is risk without benefit',
         narration='The program prints the same sections. [[slnc 400]] First, what the job needs: two permissions. [[slnc 400]] Before the fix, a stolen token reaches fifteen of fifteen, and thirteen of them the job never needs. [[slnc 500]] After the fix, two of fifteen, and none wasted. [[slnc 500]] And an admin with an explicit deny on the secrets still reaches thirteen. [[slnc 300]] Better, but still far too much for one job.'),
    dict(key='11-act-3-figure', kind='figure', title='Two people for a refund: the requests and the results',
         body='refund_steps',
         narration="Third demo: separation of duties. [[slnc 400]] At the top, the rule. [[slnc 300]] To approve a refund you need the permission to approve, and you may not be the person who created it. [[slnc 600]] On the left, in blue, five requests, in order. [[slnc 300]] On the right, what the refund desk said to each. [[slnc 600]] One. Alice, in support, creates refund R one. [[slnc 300]] Done. [[slnc 400]] Two. Alice tries to approve it. [[slnc 300]] Not allowed: support cannot approve. [[slnc 400]] Three. Dave, an admin, creates refund R two. [[slnc 300]] Done. [[slnc 400]] Four. Dave tries to approve his own refund. [[slnc 300]] As an admin he has every permission. [[slnc 300]] Still refused: same person. [[slnc 400]] Five. Carol, in finance, approves Alice's refund. [[slnc 300]] Done. [[slnc 600]] Nobody can move money alone. [[slnc 300]] So one stolen account is not enough."),
    dict(key='12-act-3', kind='console', title='Act Three — Separation of duties: two people for a refund',
         body='THREE. Separation of duties: two people for a refund.\n  THE RULE: approving needs refunds:approve, and not the creator\n  THE REQUESTS (who asks for what):\n    1  alice (support)  creates refund R-1\n    2  alice (support)  approves refund R-1\n    3  dave (admin)     creates refund R-2\n    4  dave (admin)     approves refund R-2\n    5  carol (finance)  approves refund R-1\n  THE RESULTS (what the refund desk says):\n    1  DONE\n    2  NOT_ALLOWED  support has no refunds:approve\n    3  DONE\n    4  SAME_PERSON  dave created R-2  <- ADMIN POWER DOES NOT HELP\n    5  DONE  R-1 approved: true\n  nobody can move money alone: one stolen account is not enough',
         narration='Here is the output. [[slnc 400]] The rule first. [[slnc 300]] Then the five requests, in one section. [[slnc 400]] Then the five results, in another, with the same numbers. [[slnc 500]] Done. [[slnc 200]] Not allowed. [[slnc 200]] Done. [[slnc 200]] Same person. [[slnc 200]] Done. [[slnc 500]] And refund R one ends up approved, by a second person.'),
    dict(key='13-act-4-figure', kind='figure', title='A grant that ends by itself',
         body='timeline',
         narration="Fourth demo: access that expires. [[slnc 400]] Along the line is the demo's clock. [[slnc 300]] Each step is called a tick. [[slnc 600]] At the top, in blue, the grant. [[slnc 300]] Carol, in finance, gets permission to write payments, for a month end fix. [[slnc 300]] It starts at tick ten, and it ends at tick thirteen. [[slnc 300]] Thirteen is the first tick without it. [[slnc 400]] The green bar shows when she holds it. [[slnc 600]] Below, in amber, four checks. [[slnc 300]] Can Carol write payments? [[slnc 400]] At tick nine, before the grant: false. [[slnc 300]] At tick ten: true. [[slnc 300]] At tick twelve: still true. [[slnc 300]] At tick thirteen: false again. [[slnc 600]] Nobody had to remember to take it back. [[slnc 300]] The end was written into the grant, on the day it was given."),
    dict(key='14-act-4', kind='console', title='Act Four — Access that expires by itself',
         body='FOUR. Access that expires by itself.\n  THE GRANT (for a month-end fix):\n    carol (finance) gets payments:write\n    from tick 10 until tick 13, the first tick without it\n  THE CHECKS (can carol write payments?):\n    tick 9   false  before the grant\n    tick 10  true   inside the grant\n    tick 12  true   inside the grant\n    tick 13  false  expired by itself\n  nobody had to remember to remove it',
         narration='The output shows the same two sections. [[slnc 400]] The grant: who, what, from when, and until when. [[slnc 500]] Then the checks, one per line: the tick, the answer, and why. [[slnc 400]] False, true, true, false.'),
    dict(key='15-act-5-figure', kind='figure', title='Unused permissions, and owner-only files',
         body='creep_and_files',
         narration='Fifth demo: privilege creep, and the operating system. [[slnc 400]] Privilege creep is permission that piles up, and is never taken away. [[slnc 600]] On the left, in amber, the audit. [[slnc 300]] Today is day two hundred. [[slnc 300]] Anything not used in the last ninety days counts as unused. [[slnc 300]] Two hundred minus ninety is one hundred and ten. [[slnc 300]] So anything last used before day one hundred and ten. [[slnc 500]] Five permissions are held. [[slnc 300]] Bob has never used orders read. [[slnc 300]] Carol last read payments on day one hundred. [[slnc 300]] Those two, in red, are candidates for removal. [[slnc 700]] On the right, the same idea for a file. [[slnc 300]] File permissions are nine letters, in three groups of three: the owner, the group, and everyone else. [[slnc 300]] R is read, w is write, and a dash means not allowed. [[slnc 500]] Before the fix, the export file is r w dash, r dash dash, r dash dash. [[slnc 300]] Everyone on the machine can read it. [[slnc 500]] After the fix, r w, then six dashes. [[slnc 300]] Only the owner can.'),
    dict(key='16-act-5-1', kind='console', title='Act Five — Privilege creep, and the operating system',
         body='FIVE. Privilege creep, and the operating system.\n  THE HOLDINGS (5 permissions, and when each was last used):\n    alice  orders:read      last used on day 198\n    alice  customers:read   last used on day 150\n    bob    inventory:write  last used on day 190\n    bob    orders:read      never used\n    carol  payments:read    last used on day 100\n  THE AUDIT (unused for 90 days: before day 200 - 90 = 110):\n    bob    orders:read      never used            <- REMOVE\n    carol  payments:read    last used on day 100  <- REMOVE\n    2 of 5 are candidates for removal',
         narration='Here is the output. [[slnc 400]] The holdings: five permissions, and when each was last used. [[slnc 400]] Then the audit, which works out the cut off day, two hundred minus ninety, and marks the two to remove.'),
    dict(key='17-act-5-2', kind='console', title='Act Five — Privilege creep, and the operating system',
         body='FIVE. (continued)\n  BEFORE THE FIX (the export file as first written):\n    export.csv  rw-r--r--  readable by others: true\n  AFTER THE FIX (owner only):\n    export.csv  rw-------  readable by others: false\n    its folder  rwx------\n  the Java SecurityManager is disabled for good (JDK 24),\n  so the limits that remain are outside the program:\n  separate operating system users, containers, processes',
         narration='Then the file, before and after the fix. [[slnc 300]] Readable by others, true, then false, and its folder is locked too. [[slnc 500]] The Java security manager has been switched off for good, since J D K twenty four. [[slnc 300]] So the limits that remain are outside the program: separate users, containers, and separate processes.'),
    dict(key='18-ladder', kind='bullets', title='Ways To Shrink Privilege',
         body=['Smaller roles', 'Deny by default, explicit deny wins', 'Two people for risky actions', 'Access that expires', 'Remove what is not used'],
         narration="Let's put the ways side by side. [[slnc 400]] Smaller roles. [[slnc 500]] Deny by default, and an explicit deny that wins. [[slnc 500]] Two people for risky actions. [[slnc 500]] Access that expires. [[slnc 500]] And removing what is not used."),
    dict(key='19-recognise', kind='bullets', title='Where You Have Seen It',
         body=['Cloud IAM policies', 'Kubernetes roles, sudo rules', 'Read-only database users, chmod 600', 'Four-eyes approval'],
         narration='You have probably met this already. [[slnc 400]] Cloud I A M policies, for identity and access management, have an explicit deny and a default deny. [[slnc 500]] Kubernetes has roles and role bindings, and Unix has sudo rules. [[slnc 500]] Read only database users, and the command chmod six hundred, are the same idea. [[slnc 500]] And four eyes approval for payments is separation of duties.'),
    dict(key='20-verdict', kind='bullets', title='How To Use It',
         body=['Start with admins and unattended jobs', 'Measure the blast radius of each credential', 'Two people for money and secrets', 'Prefer temporary access', 'Review what is used'],
         narration='So, how do you use it? [[slnc 400]] Start with administrators and unattended jobs. [[slnc 500]] Measure the blast radius of each credential. [[slnc 500]] Require two people for money and for secrets. [[slnc 500]] Prefer temporary access to permanent access. [[slnc 500]] And review what is really used.'),
    dict(key='21-classes', kind='bullets', title='The Java Classes',
         body=['Policy: allow and deny statements, and the decision', "Catalogue: the store's 15 permissions, and its roles", 'Access: who has which roles, and grants that expire', 'RefundDesk: a refund needs two different people', 'UsageAudit: finds permissions nobody has used lately', 'OwnerOnly: sets operating system file permissions'],
         narration='Now, a quick look at the code. [[slnc 300]] Six small classes do the work, and the demo class runs the five acts. [[slnc 300]] Here is what each one does.'),
    dict(key='22-class-1', kind='code', title='Policy — deny by default, and a deny wins',
         body='// Policy: allow and deny statements, and the decision\nDecision decide(String permission) {\n    boolean allowed = false;\n    for (Statement statement : statements) {\n        if (matches(statement.pattern(), permission)) {\n            if (!statement.allow()) {\n                return Decision.DENY_EXPLICIT;\n            }\n            allowed = true;\n...\n    return allowed ? Decision.ALLOW : Decision.DENY_DEFAULT;',
         narration='A policy is a list of allow and deny statements. [[slnc 300]] Decide goes through them one by one. [[slnc 300]] A matching deny returns at once: deny explicit. [[slnc 300]] A matching allow only remembers it. [[slnc 300]] If nothing matched, the answer is deny by default.'),
    dict(key='23-class-2', kind='code', title='Catalogue — roles are bundles of permissions',
         body='// Catalogue: the store\'s 15 permissions, and its roles\nstatic final Map<String, Policy> ROLES = Map.of(\n        "admin", Policy.of("allow *"),\n...\n        "exporter", Policy.of("allow orders:read",\n                "allow reports:read"));',
         narration='The catalogue lists the fifteen permissions, each written as resource, colon, action. [[slnc 300]] Each role is a policy. [[slnc 300]] Admin is a single allow star. [[slnc 300]] The exporter, for the nightly job, allows just two.'),
    dict(key='24-class-3', kind='code', title='Access — a grant only counts inside its window',
         body='// Access: who has which roles, and grants that expire\nfor (Grant grant : grants.getOrDefault(user, List.of())) {\n    boolean inside = tick >= grant.from() && tick < grant.until();\n    if (inside && Policy.matches(grant.permission(), permission)) {\n        return true;',
         narration="Access joins a user's roles into one policy. [[slnc 300]] An explicit deny still wins. [[slnc 300]] A temporary grant counts only from its first tick, up to but not including its last. [[slnc 300]] That is why tick thirteen says false."),
    dict(key='25-class-4', kind='code', title='RefundDesk — permission first, then not the creator',
         body='// RefundDesk: a refund needs two different people\nif (!access.can(user, "refunds:approve", tick)) {\n    return Outcome.NOT_ALLOWED;\n}\nif (creators.get(refund).equals(user)) {\n    return Outcome.SAME_PERSON;\n}',
         narration='The refund desk remembers who created each refund. [[slnc 300]] To approve, you need the permission. [[slnc 300]] And you must not be the creator. [[slnc 300]] An admin passes the first check, and still fails the second.'),
    dict(key='26-class-5', kind='code', title='UsageAudit — never used, or too long ago',
         body='// UsageAudit: finds permissions nobody has used lately\nstatic List<Holding> unused(List<Holding> holdings, long today, long days) {\n    return holdings.stream()\n            .filter(h -> h.lastUsedDay() < 0 || today - h.lastUsedDay() > days)\n            .toList();',
         narration='Each holding is a user, a permission, and the day it was last used. [[slnc 300]] Minus one means never. [[slnc 300]] A holding is unused if it was never used, or if more than ninety days have passed.'),
    dict(key='27-class-6', kind='code', title='OwnerOnly — lock a file to its owner',
         body='// OwnerOnly: sets operating system file permissions\nstatic String lockFile(Path file) throws IOException {\n    Set<PosixFilePermission> ownerOnly = PosixFilePermissions.fromString("rw-------");\n    Files.setPosixFilePermissions(file, ownerOnly);',
         narration="Owner only uses the operating system's own permissions. [[slnc 300]] The Java standard library can set them directly. [[slnc 300]] Read and write for the owner, and nothing for anyone else."),
    dict(key='28-outro', kind='outro', title='Thanks for Watching',
         body=['Full source, notes, diagrams and an animated walkthrough', 'are in the repository. Add a refund-reviewer role', 'and count what it reaches.'],
         narration="That's the principle of least privilege. [[slnc 400]] If you remember one sentence, make it this one. [[slnc 300]] Give only the access that is needed, for only as long as it is needed, so one stolen account reaches as little as possible. [[slnc 500]] The full source code, written notes, diagrams, and an animated walkthrough are all in the repository. [[slnc 300]] It runs on your own machine, with nothing installed except a Java development kit. [[slnc 500]] Here is one exercise to try. [[slnc 300]] Add a new role, and count how many permissions it reaches. [[slnc 500]] If you found this useful, a like really helps other people discover it. [[slnc 300]] Kindly subscribe to the channel. [[slnc 300]] It helps the channel grow and bring you more content. [[slnc 400]] Thanks for watching!"),
]

VIDEO = {'footer': 'The Principle of Least Privilege  ·  Security Basics  ·  Java', 'poster': {'headline': [('LEAST', 'text'), ('PRIVILEGE', 'accent')], 'taglines': [('Give only the access that is needed, for only as long as it is needed', 'gold')], 'before': {'code': 'role = admin', 'note': '15 of 15 reachable', 'tag': 'BEFORE'}, 'after': {'code': 'role = exporter', 'note': '2 of 15 reachable', 'tag': 'AFTER'}}, 'highlight': {'console_green': ['AFTER'], 'console_rose': ['BEFORE', 'THE DECISION'], 'console_amber': ['THE AUDIT', 'THE RESULTS', 'THE CHECKS'], 'console_cyan': ['THE REQUEST', 'THE CATALOGUE', 'THE ROLES', 'THE GRANT', 'THE HOLDINGS', 'THE CHECK', 'THE RULE', 'WHAT THE', 'ANOTHER']}}
